  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Still a few showers/storms in the Laurels today,\nbut dry and becoming less humid elsewhere.\n\nA quasi-stationary front is draped across Central PA this\nmorning, supporting a broken deck of stratocumulus clouds and a\nfew isolated showers. This front will continue to sag southwest\ntoday thanks to a push of northeasterly winds at the surface.\nTo the southwest of this boundary in the Laurel Highlands, there\nwill still be a few rounds of showers and storms there today.\nA complex of thunderstorms is moving southeast through Central\nOhio early this morning and is progged to bring showers/storms \nto southwest PA this morning into the early afternoon.\n\nAn upper low will gradually impinge on northeast PA today, and\nthose relatively cooler temperatures aloft will support widespread\nstratocumulus clouds. Highs this afternoon will still be a few \ndegrees warmer than average for this time of year, but dewpoints\nwill decrease this afternoon and evening. Considerable relief \narrives tonight as dewpoints and low temperatures drop into the\n50s areawide (and touch the upper 40s in the northwest \nmountains). There could be some fog tonight as well, especially\nacross the northeast part of the area.\n\n   -------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A fall preview on Sunday & Labor Day precedes another\nbrief uptick in heat and humidity next week.\n\nHigh pressure will build into the region tomorrow and Monday,\nushering in much-needed relief from an incredibly active start \nto the month of September. Other than some morning valley fog, \nthe weather will be really nice for the Sunday and Labor Day \nMonday. Highs mainly in the 70s and lows 45-55F both Sunday \nnight and Monday night will be ideal for outdoor activities. \nSunday could end up being mostly cloudy as the base of an upper\nlow rotates through the region and a few isolated showers are\npossible. High pressure builds in on Monday, ensuring abundant\nsunshine for Labor Day. There is a strong signal for widespread\nvalley fog on Monday morning with high pressure overhead\nproviding ideal conditions for optimal radiational cooling.\n\nSurface high pressure moves east of us by Tuesday, which means \nsouthwest winds will lead to an uptick in heat and humidity once\nagain. Upper level ridging will nudge into the northeast by the\nmiddle of next week bringing continued dry weather. Heat index\nvalues back in the 80s to low 90s will peak on Wednesday.\nFortunately, the \"heat\" looks short lived and we'll see\ntemperatures cooling off again for the end of the week.",
